<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fannie Lou Hamer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fannie Lou Hamer was an American voting and women's rights activist, community organiser and leader of the civil rights movement. A plantation timekeeper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, she was fired after trying to register to vote in 1962 and became a field secretary of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. Beaten almost to death in jail after an arrest at Winona in June 1963, she went on to help organise the Freedom Summer. As vice-chair of the Mississippi Freedom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> she gave televised testimony to the credentials committee of the 1964 Democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fannie-lou-hamer.jpg" alt="Fannie Lou Hamer" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fannie_Lou_Hamer_1964-08-22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fannie Lou Hamer was born Fannie Lou Townsend on 6 October 1917 in Montgomery County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, the last of the 20 children of Lou Ella and James Lee Townsend. In 1919 the family moved to Ruleville to work as sharecroppers on W. D. Marlow's plantation. From the age of six she picked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and at 12 she left school to help support her aging parents. In 1944, after the plantation owner discovered that she could read, she was made the plantation's time and record keeper. The next year she married Perry Hamer, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractor</a> driver on the plantation, and they stayed there for 18 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 31 August 1962 Hamer and 17 others tried to register to vote and failed a literacy test. She took the test again and passed it on 10 January 1963, but her county also required two poll tax receipts, which she later paid for and obtained. After her first attempt she was fired, and on 10 September 1962 she was shot at 15 times in a drive-by shooting; no one was injured. In early 1963 she became a field secretary of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee for voter registration and welfare programs.</p><p>On 9 June 1963 she was arrested at Winona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, with fellow activists returning from a workshop. In the county jail police ordered two inmates to beat her with a baton, and she was almost killed.</p><p>In 1964 she co-founded the Mississippi Freedom Democratic Party. At the Democratic National Convention that summer its members stood as Mississippi's official delegation. Hamer's televised testimony to the Credentials Committee was interrupted by an impromptu press conference by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a>, but most major networks broadcast it in full that evening. The party rejected a compromise offering two seats, and it was seated at the 1968 convention. She ran for the U.S. House in 1964 and for the Mississippi State Senate in 1971, and in 1969 she began the Freedom Farm Cooperative, an attempt to redistribute economic power among Black farmers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hamer published her autobiography in 1967 with the help of Julius Lester and Mary Varela. In 1970 she led legal action against the government of Sunflower County for continued illegal segregation, and in 1972 she was elected a national party delegate. She also co-founded the National Women's Political Caucus. She died on 14 March 1977, aged 59, in Mound Bayou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, and Andrew Young delivered the eulogy at her memorial service. She was inducted into the National Women's Hall of Fame in 1993, and in January 2025 President Joe Biden posthumously awarded her the Presidential Medal of Freedom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fannie Lou Hamer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Nashville sit-ins and kept the Freedom Rides going; Bob Moses led SNCC’s voter registration work in Mississippi; and <b>Fannie Lou Hamer</b> (1917–1977), a sharecropper thrown off her plantation for trying to register, was beaten in a Mississippi jail and described her ordeal before the credentials committee of the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahalia Jackson's 1947 recording was widely broadcast, and during the civil rights movement she sang it for marchers; in 1964 <b>Fannie Lou Hamer</b> led marchers in Mississippi singing it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>It was included in the 1968 compilation Freedom Is a Constant Struggle: Songs of the Freedom Movement, and the Staple Singers' version became part of the civil rights movement; <b>Fannie Lou Hamer</b> also sang it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montgomery County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Mound Bayou, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.5,-89.61&amp;year=1956" aria-label="Montgomery County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2261 948 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fannie Lou Hamer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2711 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2711 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.5,-89.61&amp;year=1956">What was here in 1956? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
